     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 7,650 mg/day of metformin is three times the maximum labeled dose. I know of no clinical indication for it, and it could cause life-threatening lactic acidosis in a patient with normal kidney function. Confirming the dose doesn't change that risk for the patient.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **If the intended dose was 2,550 mg/day,** I can enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals right now.\n- **If you want a different dose,** give me the dose and frequency and I'll enter it. For a new start, that would usually be 500 mg once or twice daily, titrated up.\n- **If you believe a dose above the labeled maximum is warranted,** please discuss it with your attending and pharmacy first. If they approve, it can be entered through that pathway with their documentation in the order note.\n\nWhich of these would you like?"
      }
